# census-concierge — guardrails

Conversational discovery for the Census table ecosystem. Users are GIS
professionals and non-profit researchers who are **expert in a handful of tables
and lost in the other thousand**. The product is *discovery*.

Always read before any non-trivial change: `CLAUDE.md` · `.claude/DESIGN.md` ·
`.claude/PLAN.md`. Then the catalog that matches the work — not all of them
every turn:

- Current shape: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`
- Response, URL, Census interpretation, UI rendering, or review:
  `docs/requirements.md`
- `POST /ask` behavior: `docs/ask-path.md`
- Index, search, rerank, or retrieval eval: `docs/retrieval.md`
- Scope or status: `docs/slices.md`

Slice work follows `docs/playbooks/run-slice.md`: pre-flight claim check, Gate 1
(tests + mutation check), Gate 2 (cold review), E2E before and after merge. Every
phase persists its output to git / the PR / `evidence/slice-<N>/` before the next
begins; a fresh context rebuilds from those, never from chat history.

## Budgets — the one rule that matters

`budgets.toml` caps lines, files, graph nodes, tools, models, dependencies and
latency, and sets floors on retrieval and answered rate. `python
scripts/check_budgets.py` enforces them.

**You may never raise a budget.** If a change does not fit, stop and tell the
user which limit blocks it and why. Raising a limit is a human commit with a
written reason. This is the only mechanism protecting this project from the
failure it was built to avoid: a predecessor reached 38,000 lines across 116
files and could not answer "population of New York City" in under 75 seconds.

## Definition of done

`make demo` — real questions, real APIs, pass rate and p95 latency. Quote the
printed scoreboard; do not Read the transcript. A passing pytest run is
not done. A new test file is not done. A closed ticket is not done.

## Every response ships

Full API URL (variables, geography, vintage — **even when the fetch fails**),
margins of error, GEOID, universe, and related tables. Catalog:
`docs/requirements.md`.

## Never

- Ask a blocking clarification question. Users who do not know the ecosystem
  **cannot answer one.** Show candidates as results plus an editable plan strip.
- Add a graph node for branching. Nodes are durable checkpoints only.
- Create a module named `*_manager`, `*_orchestrator`, `*_factory`, `*_policy`,
  `*_strategy`, or `*_service`.
- Add a validator that validates another validator's output.
- Introduce an abstraction before a second caller exists. Inline it.
- Work ahead of the current slice in `.claude/PLAN.md`. If something belongs to a
  later slice, say which one and stop.
- Carry slice state in the conversation. Persist each phase to the repo; a reset
  session rebuilds from `.claude/PLAN.md` + `evidence/slice-<N>/` + the PR body.
- Ship a new test without mutation-checking it: revert the behaviour it covers,
  confirm the test fails, restore.

## When unsure

Stop and ask. Do not invent a subsystem to cover an ambiguity — that is exactly
how the predecessor grew a 43-file clarification layer around a retriever that
did not work.
